<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- ★★ REQUIRED FOR SDRPLAY. The hardened runtime enforces LIBRARY VALIDATION: a signed app
       may only load libraries signed by the same Team ID (or Apple). SDRplay's API is a
       separate install signed by SDRPLAY LIMITED, so without this entitlement dlopen() is
       refused and RSPs simply never appear — on a NOTARISED build only. It works perfectly in
       an unsigned dev build, which is exactly what makes it a trap: the failure appears only
       in the artefact you ship (2026-07-27).
       ★ The narrow alternative (disable-library-validation's cousin, allow-dyld-environment-
       variables) does not help here; library validation is the specific check that bites. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- ★★★ AN ICOM'S RECEIVE AUDIO (docs/ICOM-SUPPORT-PLAN.md, Phase 2). The radio's USB codec is an audio
       INPUT, and under the hardened runtime CoreAudio input is refused outright without this entitlement —
       no prompt, just silence, and only in the NOTARISED build (the same trap as the one above). With it,
       macOS asks the owner once (NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in the Info.plist). Capture only: VibeServer
       never plays into the radio. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
